column2:
Alvin had 20 oranges. He gave some oranges to Roy and had 8 left. Roy had 16 oranges. How many oranges did Alvin give to Roy?

Let \( x \) represent the number of oranges Alvin gave to Roy. Set up an equation based on the given information and solve for \( x \).

\begin{equation*}
20 - x = 8
\end{equation*}

Solving for \( x \) gives:

\begin{equation*}
x = 20 - 8
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
x = 12
\end{equation*}

Alvin gave Roy 12 oranges.

What is the acceleration of a 2000 kg car if a 1000 N force is applied to it?

\begin{equation*}
\text{Acceleration} = \frac{\text{Force}}{\text{Mass}} = \frac{1000 \text{ N}}{2000 \text{ kg}} = \_\_\_ \text{m/s}^2
\end{equation*}

The usual selling prices of a handbag and a set of jewellery were \$990 and \$550 respectively. Jane bought them at a 10\% discount during a sale. How much did she pay altogether?

\subsection*{Answers}
Jane paid \$1,386 altogether.

Sofia has \( \frac{3}{4} \) of a cake. She divides the cake into three pieces and eats one of the pieces. What fraction of Sofia's cake is left? Show your working.

\subsection*{Answers}
Sofia has \( \frac{3}{4} \) of a cake. After dividing it into three pieces, each piece is \( \frac{3}{4} \div 3 = \frac{3}{12} = \frac{1}{4} \). After eating one piece, she has \( \frac{3}{4} - \frac{1}{4} = \frac{2}{4} = \frac{1}{2} \) of the cake left.

Copy and punctuate these direct speech sentences.
\begin{enumerate}
\item Rose said We are your new neighbours.
\item We should make them feel welcome said Father Mouse.
\item Shall we invite them for tea asked Mother Mouse.
\end{enumerate}

All the letters of these words are jumbled up. Rearrange the letters so that they make meaningful words.
\begin{enumerate}
\item neniusatal
\item stwgeee
\item eensegmnt
\item ceshrt
\item nburi
\end{enumerate}

\subsection*{Hints}

\begin{enumerate}
\item The answer starts with the letter \emph{a}\\
The meaning of this word (in English) :\\
\emph{connect}
\item The answer starts with the letter \emph{g}\\
The meaning of this word (in English) :\\
\emph{been}
\item The answer starts with the letter \emph{s}\\
The meaning of this word (in English) :\\
\emph{segment}
\item The answer starts with the letter \emph{r}\\
The meaning of this word (in English) :\\
\emph{right}
\item The answer starts with the letter \emph{b}\\
The meaning of this word (in English) :\\
\emph{brown}
\end{enumerate}

\subsection*{Answers}
\begin{enumerate}
\item aansluiten
\item geweest
\item segmenten
\item rechts
\item bruin
\end{enumerate}

Invent your own robot which would help with cleaning. Think of a name for your robot. List the main functions of your robot and the advantages of using it to do the cleaning.

\paragraph{Think With Me}
Is your robot cleaner humanoid or not? Why did you make that choice?

\paragraph{Bonus Activity}
Design a poster to advertise the new robot cleaner.

Choose an animal to research. Here are some suggestions: tiger, bee, kangaroo, tortoise.
\begin{enumerate}
\item What animal have you chosen to write about?
\item Make notes on what you already know.
\item Do your research and make notes on new information.
\end{enumerate}

Find all the possible modifications you can make to the calculation below to make it correct.
\begin{align} 
\frac{1}{3} = \frac{3}{3}
\end{align}

In the table below, write three words that use each ending: ery, ary, ory.
\begin{table}[]
\begin{tabular}{|l|l|l|}
ery & ary & ory \\
\hline
 &  &  \\
 &  &  \\
&  &  \\
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

A stamp album costs \$12.50. A photo album costs \$3.50 more than the stamp album. Find the total cost of the two albums.

\subsection*{Answers}
The total cost of the two albums is \$19.00.

Copy these sentences. Use these words to fill in the gaps: food, ears, speak, teeth, eyes.
\begin{enumerate}
  \item We use our mouth to \_\_\_ and to \_\_\_ and to breathe.
  \item We use our \_\_\_ to bite and chew our \_\_\_.
\end{enumerate}

Complete the table.
\begin{table}[]
\begin{tabular}{|l|l|l|}
\hline
Verb family name & Past simple tense & Present perfect tense \\ \hline
to invite        & we \_\_\_         & we \_\_\_             \\ \hline
to enjoy         & they \_\_\_       & they \_\_\_           \\ \hline
to call          & she \_\_\_        & she \_\_\_            \\ \hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Different kinds of pushing and pulling forces can change the shape of an object. True / False

\subsection*{Answers}
\begin{itemize}
\item True
\end{itemize}

\begin{enumerate}
\item What shape of box is used to show a logical test? Draw the shape.
\item What shape of box is used to show output? Draw the shape.
\end{enumerate}

Calculate the difference between 963 and 389.

\subsection*{Answers}
The difference is 574.

Calculate \( 837 \div 9 \).

\subsection*{Answers}
The answer is 93.

Write the plural of the following nouns:  
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Actriz
    \item Pantalones
    \item Camión
    \item Pez
    \item Jueves
\end{enumerate}

\subsection*{Answers}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Actrices
\item Pantalones
\item Camiones
\item Peces
\item Jueves
\end{enumerate}

What is the next number in this sequence?  
\begin{equation*}
   8, 6, 4, 2, 0
\end{equation*}
How do you know?

Brightness depends on light intensity. True / False

\subsection*{Answer}
\begin{itemize}
\item True
\end{itemize}

Translate the following sentences from Spanish to English.  
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Me gustan mucho sus pinturas.
    \item Conozco a una amiga tuya.
    \item ¿Cuándo celebra Mateo su cumpleaños?
    \item Pepe no encuentra sus llaves.
    \item Una prima suya vive en Nueva York.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection*{Answers}
\begin{enumerate}
\item I like his paintings very much.
\item I know a friend of yours.
\item When does Mateo celebrate his birthday?
\item Pepe can't find his keys.
\item A cousin of yours lives in New York.
\end{enumerate}

Group the animals by writing the names in the correct areas in the Carroll diagram: a sparrow, a bat, a penguin, a squirrel

\begin{table}[h!]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|}
\hline
                       & \hspace{1cm} Can Fly \hspace{1cm} & \hspace{1cm} Cannot Fly \hspace{1cm} \\ \hline
\rule{0pt}{3ex} 2 or Fewer Legs       &                        &                        \\ [2ex] \hline
\rule{0pt}{3ex} Not 2 or Fewer Legs   &                        &                        \\ [2ex] \hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Translate the following sentences from English to Spanish.  
\begin{enumerate}
    \item I am very tired.
    \item This saxophone is old.
    \item The Spanish is a difficult language.
    \item They are talkers.
    \item Charles is very poor.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection*{Answers}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Estoy muy cansado.
\item Este saxofón es antiguo.
\item El español es un idioma difícil.
\item Ellos son comunicativos.
\item Charles es muy pobre.
\end{enumerate}
